rly with some Light of the Sun context. Arguably, Geist is the one where reaching the end through growth matters most, but since I'm trying to keep a complete shuffle, <b>Demon</b> is the more interesting one of the remaining.<br /> <br /> <b>Clarity</b>-&quot;Honey, where are my keys/pants?&quot; Anyhow, Mage's relationship to vision makes that a superficially easy pick, but actually engaging with the deeper ideas....Hunter and Deviant also make for interesting relationships. Mummy also plays nicely. There's a really <i>wild</i> version of Beast that emerges from this take, but out of those, I think <b>Mummy</b>'s relationship with the Judges and everything behind and around that makes this the winner.<br /> <br /> <b>Coded Integrity</b>-Arguably, the more &quot;human&quot; the splat, the more appropriate. Mage is easy in that regards, Vampire and Geist are also considerations, as is Deviant. There's something interesting in Changeling also engaging with it. That said, <b>Mage </b>engaging with something like the Code and then falling away from it is the most interesting, I think.<br /> <br /> <b>Synergy</b>-See the overt duality argument, and be unsurprised to see Werewolf, Changeling, and Beast here, Vampire too, now that I dwell on it. Mummy to, at an odd angle. We'll go lazy and say <b>Werewolf</b>.<br /> <br /> <b>Memory</b>-Memory plays well when the sands of time should be a thing to consider, so Vampire and Demon play nice here. Changeling too, particularly since it can more immediately play into the relevances of their day to day life. Geist can get interesting with this via the lens of death. Finally, there's a whole section in a potential Chronicler's Guide for a Beast with Memory. Honestly though, there's something delicious for the fairy tale to interact with Memory, and it has thematics that can play nicely with what Clarity was doing, so I think I'd have to give this to <b>Changeling</b>.<br /> <br /> <b>Cover</b>-It's all about who's interested in drawing up lines and veils and masks and distance. Changeling and Hunter come to mind, as does Deviant. Vampire could easily translate it over into the Mask and Dirge dichotomy. Geist has some tangential relationship via Hide From Reapers, but that's a little weak. Of these, though, <b>Deviant's</b> probably the one that most benefits from having multiple of the same meter, and the way Variations can easily degrade those makes a neat tie in.<br /> <br /> <b>Satiety/Kinship</b>-Arguably another &quot;all of them case&quot;, but the combo of balancing hunger and close relationships is so obviously an ur-sample for Conviction and Loyalty to draw from that Deviant stands out. Werewolf and Mage also have some easy draws. Same with Hunter. Vampire is also a great place ot see how this archetypal take works-in fact, so much so, I'mma give it to <b>Vampire</b>.<br /> <br /> <b>Stability</b>-Just about anything that calls for constant stressors works here, so Hunter, Changeling, and Promethean are all easy picks here. Werewolf and Geist also have interesting consequences here as well. Still, <b>Hunter</b> probably the one that gets the most out of more directly interacting with their psychological state as damage, so here we are.<br /> <br /> Obviously, about half of this is sincere, if short, consideration, and the other half is really lazy spitballing.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1492487-alternative-integrity-traits-for-splats?p=1492489#post1492489\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm...<br /> <br /> <b>Vampire <span style=\"color:#252C2F\">\u2014 </span>Cover</b>: Don't let people know you are an undead predator. Twist is that 'people' includes yourself. Fake it till you make it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><b>Demon \u2014 Pilgrimage</b>: Pave your own road to Hell with good intentions. Experience the full human existence, with all its zeniths and nadirs.<br /> <br /> <b>Promethean \u2014 Stability</b>: You are unborn, incomplete, unstable. Ground your fires with hope for tomorrow, and occasionally venting out some steam.<br /> <br /> <b>Deviant \u2014 Clarity</b>: It happened, and you did it. Don't let Them tell otherwise. Take control of your own past to seize the future, this time on your terms.<br /> <br /> <b>Changeling \u2014 Memory</b>: Remember what life was like, before it all went widdershins. Remember yourself so that you become yours, not Theirs.<br /> <br /> <b>Mummy \u2014 Wisdom</b>: Reflect upon the cruelty you and your masters inflict on the world. Lament, repent, and take responsibility.<br /> <br /> <b>Mage \u2014 Satiety</b>: Feast upon the world's Mysteries and lose yourself in trance. Fast until your Obsessions take a life on their own to push you to the next Mystery.<br /> <br /> <b>Beast \u2014 Synergy</b>: Cooperate with your Horror Within such that you may better understand, and eventually shape, the stories you tell and hear about yourself.<br /> <br /> <b>Geist \u2014 Harmony</b>: Stay too dead, and you risk being stuck. Stay too alive, and you risk being powerless. Always remember you are both.<br /> <br /> <b>Werewolf \u2014 Humanity</b>: The Wolf must Hunt, but the Man must Hold. Hold on to what you held dear, even if the Hunt washes it away.</span></div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bThere are plenty of interesting combinations here. Geist - Harmony was on mind before I forgot to write it down (I guess it's a pretty straightforward one).<br /> I love Changeling - Memory and Demon - Pilgrimage.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1492487-alternative-integrity-traits-for-splats?p=1492745#post1492745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It is hard to imagine the CofD making that the official method in 3e as opposed to an optional rule. Even if there was enough call to consider it, there's plenty of other issues to address. And it goes against the CofD's style of having each game line be specialized through variant mechanics like Integrity equivalents working differently.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly. Having each game line its own set of specialized mechanics may have the benefit of better bringing out the game's themes (although some choices are debatable*) , but for crossover chronicles I miss the 1st edition template that every splat seemed to follow. Integrity/Morality traits may have held different meanings for each splat, but at least they all worked the same way.<br /> <br /> * Honestly I don't see how Changelings and Deviants tracking psychological deterioration as health boxes instead of the usual Integrity-like dots benefits in terms of game themes. Sometimes it feels like 2e just added more complexity for the sake of it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26440-moonwhisper","name":"moonwhisper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26440_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-10T08:30:23-07:00","id":1492759,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>moonwhisper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1492487-alternative-integrity-traits-for-splats?p=1492753#post1492753\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">* Honestly I don't see how Changelings and Deviants tracking psychological deterioration as health boxes instead of the usual Integrity-like dots benefits in terms of game themes. Sometimes it feels like 2e just added more complexity for the sake of it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Smaller problems that can escalate to larger problems. Mild Clarity damage can be cleared all at once through interaction with a Touchstone where severe damage is closer to actual dot-loss in other games, and Stability is built to have problems get more and more severe instead of relying on the equivalent of a dramatic failure to get the sort of issues it already models as new or worsened Scars, to say nothing of how having a variable maximum rating prioritizes pursuit of certain Traits in a way that simply buying up Integrity dots wouldn't.<br /> <br /> The two splats whose Integrity analogue is a damage track are also the two for which loss of rating is most common; Satiety and Harmony come the closest, and the former doesn't degrade that fast unless you're actively burning it up, while the latter tilts itself toward one end or the other. People already have game-psych issues with having to spend Experiences to raise Integrity in the games where it's there to be lost down to a stable level for your playstyle, and a game where casual pings to your rating are not disastrous benefits more from a framework where you don't have to weigh minor damage as &quot;I've lost a dot in a trait in this system where losing dots in traits is a pretty big deal.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-10T11:57:49-07:00","id":1492769,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1492487-alternative-integrity-traits-for-splats?p=1492734#post1492734\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Eh? Maybe from a Mirrors 2E, maybe for a Beast Chronicler's Guide (for obvious reasons), not something as a default.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair.<br /> <br /> But my point with saying that is more that making this adjustment within the current game will be a helluva lot of work, while a 3rd edition would have the possibility of reworking things with such adaptation in mind from start. As I pictured it, each game would still focus on bringing their own Integrity-loke trait, but would also come with a side-bar for adapting it to other lines as an optional rule for bringing them within this line's theme and scope of play, and another side-bar for adapting itself to previous lines' Integrity. So it <b>is</b> an optional rule, but one that is included in the core books of each line because, well, they actually aren't out of place there.<br /> <br /> Other than that, it would just be a development principle instead of written rules, to build each splat while keeping in mind that sometimes they have to work under distinct Integrity, and each Integrity keeping in mind it may be applied to distinct splats.<br /> <br /> More importantly, though, it was just a reference to the old &quot;what you want from a 3rd edition&quot; thread and I wasn't focusing too much in it. I'd love a 2e Mirrors to include such a thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-10T13:38:26-07:00","id":1492773,"title":"","content":"To add to what Satchel said, I find the 'damage track' model of things more &quot;complexity that actually adds to the game,&quot; than, &quot;complexity for complexity's sake.&quot;<br /> <br /> Humanity is.. really more complicated than it needs to be. The entire system is just a reason to roll dice that, over time, basically don't matter compared to player intentions. If you want to play a high Humanity vampire, or you want to play a vampire slowly descending to monstrosity, or a vampire that goes down to the brink and then struggles back up, the math of the system is easy enough to manipulate that the vast majority of the time you'll hit the character arc you want your PC to go through. The odds of rolling so poorly that you hit Humanity 0 are very low, and the number of Beats you earn along the way can easily pay for any Humanity dots you want to buy and possibly have Beats left over. Well before this thread I've posited that Satiety is actually a much better model for vampires and the five Condition stages can take the place of the effects of various levels of Humanity if written correctly.<br /> <br /> You can, quite easily (it's even in the VtR 1e Chroniclers Guide) simply Humanity a lot without losing anything in the end result. You only have to deal with the subjectivity of play experience (such as Humanity being rolled making it feel riskier than it really is).<br /> <br /> I'll speak to Clarity as I have way more experience with it. What the &quot;Integrity as Health&quot; model does, for the cost of some complexity, is allow a lot more variability to the immediate mental state of changelings without having to shift their &quot;rating&quot; around a lot (as happens with Harmony and Satiety as Satchel noted). Having a &quot;mental health&quot; track lets you have an idea of how much stress your changeling can endure, while treating threats to their stability as damage lets bounce around with bonuses/penalties as it comes and goes. While there's certainly some discussion to be had around the specific math of CtL 2e in this regard, the results are a lot more elastic: your character can get bent and stretched a lot under stress, but return to normal fairly quickly, but there's always the risk of getting stretch too far and snapping.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-07T07:13:58-08:00","id":1499059,"title":"","content":"I have an entire home for which this is a central change for everything major supernatural type in my Chronicles. This homebrew is listed as Frailty Chronicles in my signature.<br /> <br /> Each dot in what I call Stability Advantage (Short hand generic for listing Integrity, Wisdom, Clarity, Humanity Harmony, and Synthesis) represents a relationship with an NPC which gives mechanical benefits and is susceptible to being damaged as the PC acts monstrously and gains Atrocity. I have a whole system how maintenance of these relationships both keep the character sane and helps them avoid scenes and conditions where those that hunt monsters threaten there existence. It plays especially well with the masquerade themes, and avoiding huntsman themes, but overall I feel makes everything better. For werewolves and sin-eaters they have two 5 point Stability Advantage, one represents relationships with humans and the other minor spirits/ghost which represents aspects of the supernatural ecology of their territory (respective to the type). Thus having high harmony/synthesis is good, as it means being tied strongly to both sides of your nature, while low mean you are either untethered to one or both, with adverse effects for being low in either.<br /> <br /> I have found giving these abstract stats a face and voice, and an innate meaning advantage makes the players value them more, and that the NPC are a pool for which two or more separate PCs can have different relationships with the same individual interlink PCs and can create good drama dynamically.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 12-07-2022, 10:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-12-10T22:54:58-08:00","id":1499350,"title":"","content":"I run a Changeling game where the main characters are Fetches, which involved greatly expanding the template. I still used Integrity for them, but I could definitely see using Humanity or Cover instead. I suppose the difference would depend on whether the theme of the chronicle was more about keeping a grip on their ability to think of themselves as human, or more about their ability to pretend to play the part The Gentry assigned them. Since the Chronicle I'm running is themed around being &quot;more human than human,&quot; if I were to switch things I might go with Humanity over Cover.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When the cat's a Stray, the mice will pray...</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25851-thestray7","name":"TheStray7","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25851_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"176"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}